---
title: Tray of roast vegetables
description: Lesson 2.1. Dry heat, and why the pieces need space.
servings: 4
time: 55 minutes
tags: [cooking-course, the-oven]
lesson: "2.1"
technique: roasting
status: draft
---

> Technique: roasting. An oven cooks with hot dry air and a hot metal
> tray. Vegetables are mostly water, so the job is to drive water off
> their surfaces fast enough for them to brown before they turn soft
> all the way through.

Heat the oven to 220 °C (200 °C fan).

Cut @carrots{500%g}(peeled) into pieces about 6 cm long and 1.5 cm thick, giving each piece at least one flat side. Cut @red onions{2} into wedges through the root.

In a #large bowl{}, toss the vegetables with @oil{2%tbsp}(sunflower or rapeseed) and @salt{1%tsp} until every piece is coated.

> Why oil: it carries heat from the metal of the tray into the
> vegetable, which is what makes the side touching the tray brown.
-- source: Liz Bomze, Keith Dresser and Emily Rahravan, "How to Roast Any Vegetable", America's Test Kitchen, 2024-02-12, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/8018-how-to-roast-any-vegetable

Spread the vegetables on a #large baking tray{} in one layer, flat sides down, with a gap between the pieces. Cover the tray tightly with foil and roast for ~{15%minutes}.

> Why foil first: carrots are dense. Covered, they sit in their own
> steam, which softens them and cooks them through before the browning
> starts.

Take off the foil and roast for ~{25%minutes} more, turning the pieces once when the undersides are brown, until both sides are browned and a knife goes into a carrot easily.

> Why space: a crowded tray traps steam between the pieces, and steam
> stops browning. The foil used that on purpose; now you want the
> opposite.
-- source: Liz Bomze, Keith Dresser and Emily Rahravan, "How to Roast Any Vegetable", America's Test Kitchen, 2024-02-12, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/8018-how-to-roast-any-vegetable (foil for dense vegetables; space between pieces)

> Science: water cannot rise above 100 °C until it has turned to
> vapour, and food cooked in water or steam never passes that. A dry
> surface in an oven can climb towards the temperature of the oven.
> Browning needs that: McGee puts the caramelization of table sugar at
> about 165 °C and Maillard browning, sugars reacting with amino acids,
> perhaps 50 °C below it. Look up: caramelization, Maillard reaction,
> latent heat of vaporization.
-- source: Harold McGee, On Food and Cooking, 2nd ed., Scribner, 2004, section "High Temperatures and Dry Cooking Methods", https://app.ckbk.com/reference/food00011c14s001e001se003/high-temperatures-and-dry-cooking-methods ; OpenStax, University Physics Volume 2, 2016, section 1.5 (latent heat of vaporization of water 2256 kJ/kg; temperature constant during the phase change), https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/1-5-phase-changes

> Next time, without the recipe: any firm vegetable, cut with a flat
> side, coated in oil and salt, spread out on a hot tray. Cover dense
> ones first, then uncover and leave them until the side on the tray
> is brown.
